// The record holds five capabilities and the product has three, and they are disjoint sets.
//
// work-admission · claim-settlement · release-binding · delivery-record · document-projection
// are the ENGINE's internals. multi-harness-distribution · method-spine-and-execution ·
// plugin-conformance-and-validation-probes are what somebody installs. The first set is in
// KDL and published; the second is in Markdown and published by nobody.
//
// So `docs/releases/0.8.0/capabilities/` hands a reader the guts of the tool that produced
// the document instead of the product the document is about.

thin-slice "TS.260821.06" {
    slug "hold-the-products-own-capabilities"
    title "Publish a capability set that describes what the reader installed"
    kind "view"
    realizes "CAP.document-projection"
    from-storm "ES.260819.01"

    read-model "capabilities-and-what-they-own"
    actor "a reader of a release, who installed the product and did not build it"
    needs-read-model "what-is-currently-true"

    trigger "a reader opens the published capability set for a version and finds the engine's internals"
    outcome "the capability set published for a version names the product's capabilities, derived from records the checker reads"

    scenario #"""
        Given three capability records kept as hand-written Markdown outside the record, and five
             kept as KDL inside it, describing disjoint things
        When the set is published for a version
        Then it names what the product can do at that version, each capability tracing to the
             cluster it was derived FROM and the releases that shipped it — because a capability
             record nobody can check is the second copy this frame exists to attack, and the fact
             that this one has been correct so far is not something anyone can demonstrate.
        """#

    step "1" does="port the three Markdown capability records to the record, keeping their names"
    step "2" does="each declares its cluster, its owned events, its gate tests and its exclusion — name-a-capability applies to the product's own capabilities or it applies to nobody's"
    step "3" does="what each has shipped is DERIVED from bound releases, never restated. promoted-truth-is-derived already refuses a hand-written one"
    step "4" does="retire docs/capabilities/*.md — a living record kept in two places has one that is wrong and no way to say which"
    step "5" does="the five engine capabilities stay, and the published view distinguishes them: a reader is told which are the product and which are how it is built"

    excludes "inventing capabilities. The three are the three the product already claims; this makes \
        them checkable, and if one turns out to fail its own gate tests that is a finding, not a rewrite"
    excludes "the ADRs and RFCs under docs/. Those are ARGUMENT — a human's reasoning at a date — \
        and generating an argument from state would be inventing one"
    excludes "docs/product.md. The dashboard is a WORKING projection, composed on demand and never \
        committed (TS.260820.13). That it is committed today is a separate contradiction"

    layer "doctrine"    reaches="name-a-capability is what the product's own capabilities are held to, the same as any adopter's"
    layer "enforcement" reaches="the three are subject to every capability rule — gate tests, cluster trace, derived promotion"
    layer "docs"        reaches="docs/releases/<version>/capabilities/ describes the product at that version"

    claim "C1" text="the three product capabilities are records the checker reads, each passing the four gate tests" \
        settled-by="praxis check over the ported records, with the refusals it raised on the first run recorded in the iteration"
    claim "C2" text="what a capability has shipped is derived from bound releases and refused if hand-written" \
        settled-by="promoted-truth-is-derived, already implemented, exercised against a ported record"
    claim "C3" text="the published capability set separates what the product does from how it is built" \
        settled-by="a test asserting the view carries both sets and labels them, and that a reader asking `what can 0.8.0 do` is not answered with the engine's internals"
    claim "C4" text="docs/capabilities/*.md are retired, so no capability is stated in two places" \
        settled-by="the absence of the files, plus a test that a capability named in the record and in a Markdown file under docs/ is reported"

    depends-on "TS.260821.03 — the retirement shape (state · retired-at · stands-at) is declared on the schema there"

    note "the retirement shape came from TS.260821.03's schema amendment, not from TS.260821.04's \
        use of it. Naming 04 here would have been the same preference-as-dependency mistake that \
        deadlocked TS.260821.05"

    useful-alone "`what can this version of Praxis do` becomes answerable from the record. Today the \
        answer is in three Markdown files nothing checks, and the published set answers a different \
        question confidently"

    attacks "S7"

    cut-from "the ask to regenerate docs/<version> from state rather than by regex" \
        note="the request was about GENERATION. The blocker turned out to be upstream of it: the \
            generator works, and it has been generating the wrong subject"

    principle-note "this frame moved the engine's own state into a checked record eighteen \
        iterations ago and left the product's state in prose the whole time. The tool was pointed \
        outward before it was pointed at the thing it ships"

    proves "a reader of a published release is told what the product does, from records that were checked"
    does-not-prove "that the three capabilities are the RIGHT decomposition. It makes them checkable, \
        never correct — and the four gate tests are what will say"
}
